import json
MAD="MadCLIP: Few-shot Medical Anomaly Detection with CLIP"; RECAP="Beyond Static Anchors: Bounded Prototype Conditioning for Language-Free Medical Anomaly Detection"
MLCA="Fine-Tuning CLIP Models with Convolutional Features for Medical Anomaly Detection"; FED="Fed-LoRA: Cross-Domain Generative Knowledge Feedback for Few-Shot Medical Anomaly Detection"
SFAD="Bridging Vision Foundation Model Priors with CLIP for Spatial-aware Few-shot Anomaly Detection in Medical Images"
AA="AA-CLIP: Enhancing Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection via Anomaly-Aware CLIP"; PSMP="PSMP-CLIP: Patch-Prompt SAM and Multi-Semantic Prompting for CLIP-Based Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection"
GAN="G-Anomaly: A Pyramid Graph Transformer-Based Vision-Language Model for General Industrial Anomaly Detection"
PA=("Leave-one-modality-out zero-shot training on BMAD (RESC and OCT17 held out together); pixel metrics on held-out BrainMRI, LiverCT, RESC; checkpoints and thresholds selected on source validation only; "
    "mask-supervised MVFA re-run under the same rule; 3 seeds, mean and std. ")
PB=("Leave-one-modality-out on BMAD (RESC and OCT17 held out together), MVFA retrained with source-validation selection; K in {2,4,8,16}; 3 seeds x 3 support draws. Baselines: transductive MVFA pipeline, support z-score. ")
O={}
def off(tag,t,s,h,r,m,e,p):
    O[tag]="```json\n"+json.dumps({"Title":t,"Summary":s,"Hypothesis":h,"Rationale":r,"Method":m,"Experiment":e,"Provenance":p},indent=1,ensure_ascii=False)+"\n```"

off("stage3/A1/g2/o5/out_of_box",
 "A learned pseudo-mask refiner transferred from the masked source datasets to the unmasked ones",
 "In label-cost settings where some source datasets keep their masks, train a small refinement network that maps (image, coarse patch pseudo-mask) to the true mask on those datasets, and apply it to the datasets whose masks were removed.",
 "When masks are kept on one or two source datasets, a refiner trained on them raises pseudo-mask Dice on the other masked datasets by at least 10 points over guided-filter refinement, and MVFA trained on the refined masks recovers at least 95% of the full mask-supervised pixel AUROC with masks on a single dataset.",
 "The guided-filter idea refines pseudo-masks with a fixed, hand-designed edge prior. By analogy with learned image restoration, where a network trained on paired data outperforms fixed filters, the label-cost curve of the problem itself provides paired data: in every condition except 'no masks at all', some source datasets still have masks, so the mapping from coarse pseudo-mask to true mask can be learned and transferred. Lesion boundaries share generic properties across modalities (contrast edges, compactness), so a small refiner may transfer, and the question of how much one masked dataset is worth becomes sharper.",
 "1) Coarse input: continuous label-restricted patch score u (bilinear to 240 px) concatenated with the normalised image. 2) Refiner: U-Net with 4 levels, 16 base channels (~0.5 M parameters), trained on abnormal images of the still-masked source datasets with Dice + BCE against true masks, 40 epochs, Adam lr 1e-3, flips and rotations. 3) Apply to abnormal images of datasets whose masks were removed; threshold 0.5. 4) dataset/medical_zero.py uses true masks where kept and refined pseudo-masks elsewhere; train_zero.py unchanged. 5) In the 'no masks' condition the refiner is unavailable and guided filtering is used (reported separately).",
 PA+"Conditions: masks kept on 2, 1, 0 source datasets; refined vs guided-filter vs raw pseudo-masks for the removed ones. Metrics: pseudo-mask Dice on the removed datasets, trained pixel AUROC, Dice. Falsified if the refiner does not beat guided filtering by 10 Dice points with two masked datasets, or by any margin with one.",
 [MLCA])

off("stage3/A1/g2/o2/out_of_box",
 "Shift-ensemble pseudo-masks: sub-patch grid jitter doubles the effective resolution of MVFA's pseudo-masks",
 "Compute label-restricted pseudo-mask scores on four copies of each abnormal image shifted by half a patch (0 or 7 pixels in x and y), realign and average them, so the effective pseudo-mask resolution becomes 7 pixels instead of 14.",
 "Averaging four half-patch-shifted pseudo-mask score maps raises pseudo-mask Dice against true masks by at least 6 points over single-grid pseudo-masks, with the largest gain on small BrainMRI lesions, and MVFA trained on them recovers at least 3 points more of the mask-supervised pixel AUROC.",
 "The guided-filter idea borrows resolution from the raw image's edges. A different analogy, from sub-pixel imaging and multi-frame super-resolution, is that the resolution limit of the 17x17 patch grid (14 pixels per patch at 240 px, a verified property of MVFA's ViT-L/14 setting) can be overcome by sampling the grid at several offsets. Shifting the input by half a patch changes which pixels fall into each patch, so averaging realigned scores yields a map at twice the grid resolution using only CLIP itself, with no edge assumptions that fail in low-contrast modalities.",
 "1) For each abnormal source image and shift (dx, dy) in {0, 7}^2: pad by 7 px (reflect), crop the shifted 240x240 window, extract frozen CLIP layer-24 patch features, compute NN distances to the dataset's normal bank (also built from the four shifts of normal images). 2) Map each patch score back to its pixel footprint in the unshifted frame; average the four maps (each pixel covered by four patches). 3) Threshold at the 99th percentile of the same shift-averaged scores on normal source-validation images. 4) train_zero.py unchanged. Cost: 4x feature extraction for pseudo-mask generation only (one-off).",
 PA+"Conditions: true masks; single-grid pseudo-masks; 4-shift and 9-shift (0, 5, 10 px) ensembles. Metrics: pseudo-mask Dice per dataset and per lesion-size tertile, trained pixel AUROC, Dice. Falsified if the 4-shift ensemble does not raise pseudo-mask Dice by 6 points or brings no gain on the smallest-lesion tertile.",
 [MLCA, SFAD])

off("stage3/A1/g2/o6/out_of_box",
 "Occlusion attribution of MVFA's detection branch as pseudo-masks for its segmentation branch",
 "Train the detection branch on image-level labels, then mark as anomalous the patches whose replacement by their nearest normal-bank feature most reduces the detection branch's image score, and use these attribution maps as segmentation targets.",
 "Occlusion-attribution pseudo-masks from the image-label-trained detection branch achieve at least 8 points higher Dice against true masks than nearest-neighbour pseudo-masks on at least two of the three masked datasets, and MVFA trained on them recovers at least 90% of the mask-supervised pixel AUROC.",
 "The veto idea uses the detection branch's map as a second opinion. By analogy with counterfactual explanation in weakly supervised localisation, the detection branch, trained with reliable image labels on all six datasets, implicitly knows which patches make an image abnormal: replacing such a patch by its closest normal counterpart should lower the image score. This counterfactual occlusion gives a causal attribution rather than a correlational map, uses only components that MVFA already has (det_adapters and a normal bank), and directly targets the problem's question of what image-level labels can teach the segmentation branch.",
 "1) Stage 1: train det_adapters only (train_zero.py, seg loss off) on all six sources, 20 epochs, selected on source-validation image AUROC. 2) For each abnormal source image: features F per level; for each patch p, F_p' = F with patch p replaced by its nearest neighbour in the dataset's normal bank at each level; attribution a_p = score(F) - score(F_p') with score the det branch's mean-pooled anomaly output (batched over patches: 289 forward passes of the adapters only, CLIP features cached). 3) Pseudo-mask = a_p > 99th percentile of a on normal source-validation images (where attributions should be small). 4) Stage 2: train seg branch on these masks with Focal + Dice; det branch continues. Hyper-parameters as MVFA.",
 PA+"Conditions: true masks; NN pseudo-masks; occlusion pseudo-masks; Grad-CAM of the det branch (cheaper attribution baseline). Metrics: pseudo-mask Dice, trained pixel AUROC, Dice, image AUROC. Falsified if occlusion masks do not beat NN pseudo-masks by 8 Dice points on two masked datasets or do not beat Grad-CAM.",
 [MAD, RECAP])

off("stage3/A1/g2/o4/substitution",
 "Mean-teacher online refinement of pseudo-masks during MVFA training",
 "Replace the two discrete self-training rounds with continuous refinement: an exponential-moving-average copy of the segmentation adapters predicts maps on abnormal training images, and targets are updated each epoch as a confidence-weighted blend of the initial pseudo-mask and the teacher's prediction.",
 "Mean-teacher online refinement reaches the pseudo-mask quality of two-round self-training (Dice within 2 points) in a single training run, and the final model recovers at least as much of the mask-supervised pixel AUROC on held-out masked modalities as the two-round procedure at half the compute.",
 "The parent's framing is kept: MVFA's adapted features should localise lesions better than frozen CLIP, so the model can improve its own targets. The technique changes from discrete rounds (two full trainings) to the mean-teacher scheme used in semi-supervised segmentation, in which a slowly moving average of the model provides stable targets during a single run. The blend with the initial pseudo-mask and the label restriction to abnormal images keep the targets anchored, which addresses the parent's risk of confirmation bias, and the compute halves.",
 "1) Initial targets y0 from the label-restricted rule (frozen CLIP). 2) Teacher: EMA of seg_adapters with decay 0.99 per step. 3) From epoch 10, every epoch: teacher prediction q on each abnormal training image; target y_t = (1 - alpha_t) y0 + alpha_t q, alpha_t ramped linearly from 0 to 0.5 by epoch 30; pixels with |y_t - 0.5| < 0.15 ignored. Normal images keep zero targets. 4) Loss: Focal + Dice against y_t (soft Dice). 5) Det branch unchanged. 6) Decay and alpha schedule selected on source validation. Other hyper-parameters as MVFA.",
 PA+"Conditions: true masks; static pseudo-masks; two-round self-training (parent); mean-teacher online refinement. Metrics: pseudo-mask Dice over epochs, trained pixel AUROC, Dice, GPU hours. Falsified if mean-teacher refinement falls more than 2 pseudo-mask Dice points or 1 pixel-AUROC point below two-round self-training.",
 [SFAD, MLCA])

off("stage3/A1/g2/o1/out_of_box",
 "Size-constrained training of MVFA's segmentation branch from image-level labels",
 "Train the segmentation adapters with image-level labels and an inequality constraint on the predicted anomalous area of each image (zero for normal images, within a dataset-specific range for abnormal ones), without pixel-wise pseudo-masks.",
 "Size-constrained training with area bounds estimated from label-restricted pseudo-mask statistics recovers at least 85% of the mask-supervised pixel AUROC on held-out masked modalities and matches pixel-wise pseudo-mask training within 2 points, while being insensitive (less than 1 point change) to a 2x error in the area bounds.",
 "Self-training improves pixel targets iteratively. By analogy with constrained weakly supervised segmentation (size-constrained CNN losses), the segmentation branch can instead be supervised at the level where labels are reliable, the image, plus a weak but robust prior on how much of an abnormal image is lesion. This avoids committing to any pixel-wise pseudo-mask and its boundary errors, and it directly asks what image-level labels alone can teach MVFA's segmentation adapters, which is the core of the problem.",
 "1) Area bounds per source dataset: a_min, a_max = 10th and 90th percentiles of label-restricted pseudo-mask areas on abnormal training images. 2) Predicted area A = mean_x sigmoid(map(x)) on the seg branch output. 3) Loss for abnormal images: relu(a_min - A)^2 + relu(A - a_max)^2 plus a top-k MIL term -log mean of top-1% sigmoid(map); for normal images: A^2 plus the max-pixel BCE toward 0. 4) train_zero.py: replace Focal + Dice for pseudo-labelled datasets with this loss, weight 1; det branch unchanged. 5) Sensitivity: multiply a_min, a_max by 0.5 and 2. Hyper-parameters as MVFA.",
 PA+"Conditions: true masks; pixel-wise pseudo-masks; size-constrained loss; size-constrained with perturbed bounds. Metrics: pixel AUROC, Dice, image AUROC. Falsified if size-constrained training recovers less than 75% of the mask-supervised gain or changes by more than 2 points under a 2x bound error.",
 [SFAD, MLCA])

off("stage3/B1/g2/o4/out_of_box",
 "Extreme-value (peaks-over-threshold) calibration of per-image MVFA scores",
 "Fit a generalised Pareto distribution to the upper tail of leave-one-out support-normal patch scores and score each test image by the tail probability of its highest patch scores, which extrapolates beyond the few observed support values.",
 "Generalised-Pareto tail probabilities keep image AUROC within 1 point of the transductive pipeline and give realised false-positive rates within 2 points of nominal levels down to 1%, including at K = 2 where empirical CDF and conformal p-values cannot resolve probabilities below 1/(K + 1).",
 "The CDF-isotonic idea calibrates scores non-parametrically, which works only within the range of observed reference values. By analogy with extreme-value statistics in reliability engineering and hydrology, the tail of a distribution can be modelled reliably from exceedances over a high threshold (Pickands-Balkema-de Haan theorem), so probabilities of scores beyond the support maximum, exactly the region where anomalies lie, can be estimated from a handful of normal images. Pooling patches from all supports gives hundreds of exceedances even at K = 2.",
 "1) Fused patch map m (support LOO z-scores, averaged over levels and branches; no min-max). 2) Pool LOO support patch scores; threshold u = 95th percentile; fit GPD (shape xi, scale sigma) to exceedances by maximum likelihood (scipy.stats.genpareto). 3) Patch tail probability P(m > s) = 0.05 (1 + xi (s - u)/sigma)^(-1/xi) for s > u, empirical below. 4) Image score = -log10 of the mean tail probability of the top 3 patches. 5) Image threshold from the GPD of support LOO image scores (fitted the same way). 6) test_few.py: implement; remove min-max. No training.",
 PB+"Conditions: empirical CDF; conformal; GPD tail. Metrics: image AUROC, realised FPR at nominal 5% and 1% per K, score shift under resampling. Falsified if GPD FPRs miss nominal by more than 2 points at K >= 4 or by more than 4 points at K = 2.",
 [MAD, RECAP])

off("stage3/B1/g2/o5/out_of_box",
 "Selective per-image MVFA scoring with a conformal abstention band",
 "Give each image one of three outputs, normal, abnormal or refer to a reader, by placing an abstention band on its conformal p-value, and measure the risk-coverage trade-off that a deployable MVFA would offer.",
 "With an abstention band of p in [0.05, 0.3], selective MVFA scoring reaches at least 95% sensitivity and 95% specificity on the images it does not refer, while referring at most 25% of images on held-out modalities at K >= 4, which the original transductive pipeline cannot provide because its scores have no per-image meaning.",
 "The two-guarantee idea separates false-positive control from probability calibration. By analogy with clinical triage and selective classification, a screening system does not need to decide every case: it can refer uncertain images to a radiologist. A per-image conformal p-value supports exactly this, with a guaranteed false-positive rate for 'abnormal' calls and a calibrated band for referral. Measuring risk versus coverage turns MVFA's scoring into a decision tool and quantifies its clinical usefulness, which AUC alone cannot (G11).",
 "1) Per-image conformal p-value from the fused inductive score (support LOO, as in the conformal parent). 2) Decision: abnormal if p < alpha_lo, normal if p > alpha_hi, refer otherwise; alpha_lo = 0.05; alpha_hi chosen on source episodes to give 95% negative predictive value. 3) Report coverage, selective sensitivity and specificity, and the risk-coverage curve over alpha_hi in [0.05, 0.5]. 4) test_few.py: implement; remove min-max. No training.",
 PB+"Conditions: no abstention; abstention band selected on source episodes; oracle band (upper reference). Metrics: coverage, selective sensitivity/specificity, area under the risk-coverage curve, score shift under resampling. Falsified if selective sensitivity and specificity do not both reach 95% at 75% coverage at K >= 4.",
 [MAD, RECAP, AA])

off("stage3/B1/g2/o6/substitution",
 "Weight-free fusion of MVFA branches by a Bonferroni-corrected minimum p-value",
 "Replace inverse-variance fusion weights with a weight-free rule: convert each branch to a support-based conformal p-value and score the image by twice the smaller of the two, which is valid whatever the dependence between branches.",
 "Bonferroni-min fusion matches support-reliability-weighted log-odds fusion within 0.5 image-AUROC points on held-out modalities, keeps the realised false-positive rate at or below the nominal 5%, and removes all fitted fusion parameters.",
 "The parent's framing is kept, fusion without source episodes, but inverse-variance weights estimated from K support images are themselves noisy at small K. The minimum p-value with a Bonferroni factor is the simplest valid combination of two tests under arbitrary dependence and needs no estimated weights; it lets whichever branch is more confident for a given image decide, which suits MVFA's complementary zero-shot (semantic) and few-shot (appearance) evidence.",
 "1) Zero-shot branch: conformal p0 from LOO support scores of the temperature-calibrated softmax map (top-1% mean). 2) Few-shot branch: conformal p1 from LOO support scores. 3) p = min(1, 2 min(p0, p1)); score = -log10 p. 4) Threshold p <= 0.05. 5) test_few.py: implement; remove min-max. No training, no fitted fusion parameters.",
 PB+"Conditions: equal-weight log-odds; support-reliability weights (parent); Bonferroni-min; Simes combination (less conservative). Metrics: image AUROC, realised FPR at 0.05, score shift under resampling. Falsified if Bonferroni-min loses more than 0.5 AUROC points relative to the parent or exceeds nominal FPR.",
 [AA, PSMP, MAD])

off("stage3/B1/g2/o2/out_of_box",
 "Augmentation-consistency scoring: true anomalies persist under flips and small shifts, spurious responses do not",
 "Score each test image under eight label-preserving augmentations (flips and half-patch shifts), and use a conformal p-value of the augmentation-averaged score together with its across-augmentation spread to down-weight unstable responses.",
 "Augmentation-consistency scoring improves image AUROC by at least 1 point over single-view inductive scoring on held-out modalities and reduces the AUROC loss under injected spurious spikes by at least half, while remaining test-set-invariant.",
 "The conformal-log-odds idea makes scores per-image and calibrated but still trusts a single forward pass. By analogy with double reading in radiology, where findings seen by two independent readers are trusted more, a lesion should produce a high score under any reasonable view of the image, whereas spurious high pixels from patch-grid aliasing or noise depend on the exact grid position, a verified property of MVFA's 14-pixel patches. Averaging across augmentations suppresses such responses and the spread gives a per-image uncertainty, without any test-set statistics.",
 "1) Augmentations: identity, horizontal flip, vertical flip (if anatomically valid for the modality, else omitted), and half-patch shifts (7 px in x, y, both), realigned after scoring. 2) Fused inductive map per view (support LOO z-scores, averaged); averaged map m_bar and per-pixel std s. 3) Image score = top-1% mean of m_bar - 0.5 s. 4) Conformal p-value against support LOO image scores computed the same way. 5) test_few.py: implement; remove min-max. Cost: 8x inference. No training.",
 PB+"Conditions: single view; augmentation mean; mean minus 0.5 std. Metrics: image AUROC, AUROC under 1-5 injected spikes, realised FPR at p <= 0.05, latency. Falsified if augmentation consistency does not improve AUROC by 1 point or does not halve the spike-induced loss.",
 [MAD, RECAP, AA])
